<header><p class="enc-kicker">Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Minnesang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th–14th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Minnesang was the tradition of courtly love poetry and song in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, flourishing from the twelfth to the fourteenth century. Its poets, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minnesanger">Minnesänger</a>, were often knights or courtiers who sang of devotion to an idealised noble lady. Leading figures included Reinmar, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfram-von-eschenbach">Wolfram von Eschenbach</a>, and much of the poetry survives in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-manesse">Codex Manesse</a>.</p></div>
